**Problem:** membership test / set-intersection counting — for each key, it scans the whole `data` vector to see if the key is present, counting how many keys are found.

**Complexity:** O(K·N) — quadratic in the worst case (every key misses, so every inner scan runs to the end).

**Fix:** a hash table — build a `std::unordered_set<int>` from `data` once (O(N)), then test each key in O(1) expected time, giving **O(N + K)** total. (Alternative: sort `data` and binary-search, O((N+K) log N), if you need deterministic bounds.)

```cpp
#include <unordered_set>
int lookup(const std::vector<int>& keys, const std::vector<int>& data) {
  std::unordered_set<int> s(data.begin(), data.end());
  int hits = 0;
  for (int k : keys)
    if (s.count(k)) ++hits;
  return hits;
}
```

